P:System.DateTime.Now;Take a TimeProvider and call GetUtcNow(). A clock read from a static cannot be substituted, so anything that depends on the time becomes untestable - and the local-time variants also make a stored value depend on where the process ran.
P:System.DateTime.UtcNow;Take a TimeProvider and call GetUtcNow(). The value is right; the problem is that nothing can substitute it, so a test of anything time-dependent has to wait for real time to pass.
P:System.DateTime.Today;Take a TimeProvider and derive the date from GetUtcNow(). A date read from the machine's local clock is a different date either side of midnight somewhere.
P:System.DateTimeOffset.Now;Take a TimeProvider and call GetUtcNow().
P:System.DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;Take a TimeProvider and call GetUtcNow().
T:Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.Results;Return the result and end the endpoint with ToApiResult(http). Every response leaves through one conversion, which is what stops a handler deciding for itself what a reader is told - and ToEnvelope is what drops an exception's own message, which this type's Problem overloads will happily send. Banning the type rather than the method is deliberate: Problem has three overloads, a ban naming the wrong one is silently useless, and Ok sends a bare payload with no notices for a front end to read.
T:Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http.TypedResults;The same, and the reason it is here as well: with only Results banned, TypedResults.Problem compiles and does exactly what the ban was written to prevent. A ban on one spelling is not a ban on the behaviour.
